    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n- no proper way to define operators. there's `g@` but it's a bit awkward:\n    - it uses marks but it should be function arguments.\n    - setting `'operatorfunc'` every single time is not elegant.\n    - because it's just a mapping, showcmd will show \"g@\", not the command it self.\n- commands implemented as mappings require remapping when using from another mapping.\n- dot-repeat, showcmd don't always work well with non-builtin commands.\n- there are rules that operators and motions should follow, but it's up to the author to follow them.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nan API for defining normal mode commands, like `nvim_create_user_command` for Ex commands. something like:\n```lua\nnvim_cmd_def(\"gc\",toggle_comment,{ type = { \"motion\", \"change\" } }) -- :: Motion -> Change\nnvim_cmd_def(\"x\",del_char,{ type = \"change\" }) -- :: Change\nnvim_cmd_def(\"f\",move_to_char,{ type = { \"char\", \"motion\" } })\nnvim_cmd_def(\"i\",insert_text,{ type = { \"text\", \"change\" } })\nnvim_cmd_def(\"sa\",add_surrounding,{ type = { \"motion\", \"char\", \"change\" } }) -- :: Motion -> Char -> Change\n```\n`type` is like a type annotation, which describes what it takes and what it does; a `\"change\"` would be dot-repeatable.",
